      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="timeline">A realistic timeline</h2>
  <p>
    There is no single honest week count here, and any roadmap that gives you one is guessing. What is true
    across most people who make this switch: the research and structure habit takes real time to build if you
    are starting from zero, tool fluency can happen in parallel with that, and a genuinely deep case study
    almost never survives being rushed — an interviewer's first two follow-up questions will usually expose a
    shallow one.
  </p>
  <p>
    People coming from an adjacent background — visual design, front-end development, product, or research —
    often reach a hire-ready portfolio in a focused stretch of a few months. People starting from a completely
    unrelated background, building research habits, tool fluency, and two or three deep case studies from
    scratch, more realistically need closer to six months to a year. Judge your own pace against the build
    order above, not against a stranger's LinkedIn timeline post.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>
  <p>
    Do not open one more UX course before you have picked an entry path and named the one real problem you
    are going to turn into your first case study. That single decision does more for your odds than another
    week of watching tutorial videos.
  </p>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>Pick your entry path from the three above based on your budget and timeline, not on prestige.</p>
    <p>Then start at step one of the build order: real research on a real problem, before any tool or certificate.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    If your interest sits closer to writing the words inside a product than designing its screens, the
    adjacent <a href="/blog/career-options/ux-writing-career-path-india/">UX writing career path India</a>
    guide covers that lane in detail. Moving toward earlier financial freedom through this roadmap comes down
    to the same thing it always does: the right skill mix, visible tested proof, and clear communication,
    built deliberately rather than collected at random. If you want a second opinion on whether UX design
    fits your specific situation, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    can help you map the entry path that fits your background, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a> if you are
    still deciding whether this is genuinely your lane.
  </p>

